{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-161908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/2fff2eab1f5670dfcd3351dcbb9be1b3110e6ebdcd452cf4c5a273611d454c8b.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b00c997271ee3d23862d996e470419f38af9b336212e233f8230d3dd0c562b7d"} {"question" "q-161908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "x\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y"] ["math" "xy\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/01779de50ae00cf2883bee0e021638a423989b87a69aaa17e0332c4a671a32ca.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,z,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y+\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+{∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b00c997271ee3d23862d996e470419f38af9b336212e233f8230d3dd0c562b7d"} {"question" "q-161910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "xy-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "y+(x+y)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "y-xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/c7f140ad2c5687db48171819e1efaa858701af881ebbee57ff835da569f30947.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨{z}^{2},0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b00c997271ee3d23862d996e470419f38af9b336212e233f8230d3dd0c562b7d"} {"question" "q-161901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "xy\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "y\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/0e374f18d8b87de2fddd9a2127ee84709746c7c1b75083441afcc7b6c79402fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b00c997271ee3d23862d996e470419f38af9b336212e233f8230d3dd0c562b7d"} {"question" "q-161895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{x}^{3}-6x{y}^{2}-6x"] ["math" "-4{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x"] ["math" "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x"] ["math" "2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-6x"] ["math" "4{x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+10x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/5709af87720dcb052d8ffc6cc600c5889e45849aee6bed69ec3646e43c3cefb1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,1,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3,\\,Q=1,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)\\cdot 2x-1\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b00c997271ee3d23862d996e470419f38af9b336212e233f8230d3dd0c562b7d"} {"question" "q-161911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2x+6y-1"] ["math" "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y"] ["math" "2{x}^{3}+2{y}^{3}-12xy"] ["math" "4x{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+2x-6y"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2xy-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-(0)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b00c997271ee3d23862d996e470419f38af9b336212e233f8230d3dd0c562b7d"} {"question" "q-161904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x-y"] ["math" "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+2y"] ["math" "\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b00c997271ee3d23862d996e470419f38af9b336212e233f8230d3dd0c562b7d"} {"question" "q-161905" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161905" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}+4x"] ["math" "-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x"] ["math" "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x"] ["math" "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-5x+y"] ["math" "6{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-2y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161905" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the parabolic cylinder $z={y}^{2}-2.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={y}^{2}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cylinder ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-2)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}={({y}^{2}-2)}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-{({y}^{2}-2)}^{2}\\cdot (0)+0\\cdot (2y)+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b00c997271ee3d23862d996e470419f38af9b336212e233f8230d3dd0c562b7d"} {"question" "q-161913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+x"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y"] ["math" "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1+x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1+x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 2x-0\\cdot 2y+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x-1\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b00c997271ee3d23862d996e470419f38af9b336212e233f8230d3dd0c562b7d"} {"question" "q-161907" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161907" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2x-3"] ["math" "-3x-1"] ["math" "2x-1"] ["math" "3x-y"] ["math" "y-x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161907" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0+y+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (2x)-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(0-2y{x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b00c997271ee3d23862d996e470419f38af9b336212e233f8230d3dd0c562b7d"} {"question" "q-161914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2y{x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}-y"] ["math" "-2{y}^{3}+y"] ["math" "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y"] ["math" "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-3y"] ["math" "2y{x}^{2}-y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "q-161914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/state.json" "file_sha256" "9c06349d53c9c6850cda069c5782e98283bf8c1ab8263e494471d2f136da0939"} {"question" "e-12419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/263853df5f080da778a34caacea945119099326f0ba27806d851f793416e9f92.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 0, \\, x, \\, -z^2 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nDetermine the missing expression." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/example-12419.json" "file_sha256" "8d321df26ecd94e5166bc9b721339ccb24e629352815b196a50da3a0fea4f2bf"} {"question" "e-12419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,x,-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = 0, \\qquad\nQ = x, \\qquad\nR = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/example-12419.json" "file_sha256" "8d321df26ecd94e5166bc9b721339ccb24e629352815b196a50da3a0fea4f2bf"} {"question" "e-12420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\, y, \\, 0 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinder $z=1-x^2$ and the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/example-12420.json" "file_sha256" "bb67602b0a11d23e2dbe471080ce6195955e22bd19653cdeb83f90e92eb8b611"} {"question" "e-12420" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z=g(x,y)=1-x^2$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z=h(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper parabolic cylinder $S_1.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y, \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (-2x)-y\\cdot (0)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y , \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (2x)-y\\cdot (2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/example-12420.json" "file_sha256" "bb67602b0a11d23e2dbe471080ce6195955e22bd19653cdeb83f90e92eb8b611"}] "usage" {"q-161907" [] "e-12419" [] "e-12420" []} "basis" 2683 "retractions" [[17592186198548 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F = ⟨1, 0,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z = x^{2} + y^{2}$ and the plane $z = 1 + x$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186197971 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 0, \\, x, \\, -z^2 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A$.\nDetermine the missing expression.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4144/Source/Images/020906e1438cc7bd21000dc9fa4ca331.png)"] [17592186197971 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1$. Since the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle$. As a result, we have\n$∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot ndS; = ∬_(D)⟨0, x,-z^{2}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩dA; = ∬_(D) - z^{2}dA; = ∬_(D)0dA; = 0$.\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2$.We compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂h}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}}; \\frac{∂h}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}}$\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n$P = 0, \\qquad Q = x, \\qquad R = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4$.\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS|= - ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y} + R)dA; = - ∬_(D)(-0 \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} - x \\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} + x^{2} + y^{2} - 4)dA; = ∬_(D)\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} - x^{2} - y^{2} + 4dA$.\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x y 4 - x 2 - y 2 - x 2 - y 2 + 4dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186197972 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\, y, \\, 0 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinder $z=1-x^2$ and the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186197972 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z=g(x,y)=1-x^2$ is oriented upward, and\n- the lower surface $S_2: z=h(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper parabolic cylinder $S_1$.We compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂g}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1 - x^{2}) =-2x; \\frac{∂g}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1 - x^{2}) = 0$\n\n> If we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n> $P = x, \\qquad Q = y, \\qquad R = 0$.\n> As a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n> $∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA; = ∬_(D)(-x \\cdot (-2x) - y \\cdot (0) + 0)dA; = ∬_(D)2x^{2}dA$.\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2$. We compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂h}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2x; \\frac{∂h}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2y$\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n$P = x, \\qquad Q = y, \\qquad R = 0$.\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS|= - ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y} + R)dA; = - ∬_(D)(-x \\cdot (2x) - y \\cdot (2y) + 0)dA; = - ∬_(D) - 2x^{2} - 2y^{2}dA; = ∬_(D)2x^{2} + 2y^{2}dA$.\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2x^{2}dA + ∬_(D)2x^{2} + 2y^{2}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)4 x 2 + 2 y 2dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186194190 17592186194191 17592186194192 17592186194193 17592186194194] "immutable_fields" [[17592186194189 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194189 :answer-field/id] [17592186194189 :answer-field/key] [17592186194189 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []